**Read:** [Vacuum technology for superconducting devices — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Vacuum-for-Superconducting-Devices-Chiggiato.lingua#r_2PJTDCZXJ91HF8Q1BBY9FNMWH9), the complete molecular heat-transfer derivation; [Vacuum systems — Vincent Baglin and Roberto Kersevan](../sources/Vacuum-Systems-Baglin-Kersevan.lingua#r_1K9ATEHFM7ET2H8TRHC3QNCRFN), cryo-vacuum; the shared thermal account of radiation, supports and interfaces.

Gas molecules carry energy between surfaces even when bulk convection is absent. In a free-molecular model, the rate depends on incident flux and the energy exchanged at each surface. Accommodation, gas identity, temperatures and geometry are essential; a continuum conductivity formula has a different regime. Use flux-weighted energy: molecules crossing a plane from an ideal monatomic Maxwell gas carry mean translational energy 2kBT. Derive this moment before adding internal degrees of freedom and accommodation.

For fixed temperatures and accommodation in the dilute free-molecular limit, gas-mediated heat transfer commonly scales with gas density or pressure. Radiation and solid-support conduction do not disappear when gas pressure falls. A low-pressure reading at a warm port also needs interpretation before representing conditions near a cold surface.

**Work:** Derive a molecular energy-flux estimate using explicit degrees of freedom and accommodation assumptions. Compare it with radiation and a support-conduction estimate. Identify the regime where further pumping produces little reduction in total heat load, and explain how loss of insulation vacuum changes both thermal and gas inventories.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
